1
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:09,250
Let's start with the first part: arithmetic instructions.

2
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:23,250
Arithmetic instructions, in English, are called Arithmetic Instructions — that is, arithmetic operations.

3
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:33,590
Now, they are nothing more than addition and subtraction. Note that this does not include multiplication and division, because those instructions are not part of the base integer instructions.

4
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:38,050
Multiplication and division have a dedicated M module,

5
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:40,950
that is, the multiplication and division instruction set,

6
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:43,130
the extension set and extension module,

7
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:43,890
which we will introduce to you.

8
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:45,910
They are not within the scope of this part.

9
00:00:45,910 --> 00:00:48,110
What we mainly deal with here is addition and subtraction.

10
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:52,220
The first instruction we are going to learn,

11
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:53,240
is the add instruction,

12
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,580
which is addition.

13
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:57,040
This is the most basic one.

14
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:58,500
Now, this add instruction,

15
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,080
its encoding type is R type.

16
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:06,140
So this R type is the register type.

17
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:07,620
What does register type mean?

18
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,320
It means that its operands,

19
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:14,020
its three operands are all registers.

20
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,420
You can see it here.

21
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:17,240
In terms of encoding

22
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,780
it needs to express three registers

23
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:21,260
RD, RSC, R2

24
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:24,260
Now, when translated into assembly

25
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:28,440
this is the syntax format

26
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:30,200
Note this

27
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,280
in RISC-V

28
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:32,160
the assembly

29
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:35,880
the convention is

30
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:38,220
to put RD

31
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:41,520
the register that finally holds the result

32
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:42,460
first

33
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:44,000
reversed

34
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,840
that is, the sources go at the back

35
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:47,280
the two sources at the back

36
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,600
there is such a relationship in there

37
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:52,800
within this relationship

38
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:53,520
when it reads

39
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:54,840
what it means is this

40
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:57,460
that is, when doing addition

41
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:58,040
How do we add?

42
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:04,720
We take out the value in this first RS — the register inside.

43
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:08,260
What I'm explaining right now is actually from the CPU's point of view.

44
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:10,060
If I were the CPU,

45
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:11,740
when I'm executing this instruction,

46
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:13,120
how would I carry it out?

47
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:15,740
I take a value out of RS —

48
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:16,800
that's one of the addends.

49
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:18,540
Then,

50
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:20,740
I take another value out of RS2,

51
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:23,160
and I add these two values together,

52
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:24,720
getting a result.

53
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:25,840
Now, this result —

54
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:27,020
where do I put it?

55
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,840
I put it into the RD register.

56
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:31,780
That's really all it means.

57
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,240
This is the add instruction.

58
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,420
Now, when we actually write it,

59
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:38,100
we usually use lowercase,

60
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:39,100
when we're programming.

61
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:39,900
Normally we use lowercase

62
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:40,600
when we write this syntax.

63
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:41,440
I deliberately used uppercase,

64
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:42,260
mainly because it looks nicer.

65
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:45,160
For example, x5, x6, x7.

66
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:45,980
We talked about this earlier.

67
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:47,300
Each register has a code name,

68
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:47,780
right?

69
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:48,920
So that's 5, 6, 7.

70
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:49,760
Its encoding.

71
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:50,940
As for the specific encoding,

72
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:52,320
it's actually quite simple.

73
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:53,760
If it's x5,

74
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,420
you can imagine

75
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:56,360
that the value inside

76
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:58,440
those 5 bits is just 5.

77
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,540
The value in x6 is 6.

78
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:02,620
Once you understand that,

79
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,680
you also need to be able to read it the other way around.

80
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:09,380
Now, this encoding format is R-type.

81
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:10,260
The opcode is

82
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:12,280
011 0011.

83
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:13,640
Why is it this value?

84
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:15,080
Just look it up in the table.

85
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,260
It's listed right there in that table.

86
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:18,660
I just demonstrated that briefly earlier.

87
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:19,280
Right?

88
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:20,420
And then,

89
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:21,800
function3 is set to 000.

90
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:24,260
function7 is set to seven 0s.

91
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:25,340
Now why is that?

92
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:27,920
Actually, we may have gone through this a bit quickly just now.

93
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:29,360
Let's go back to that table again.

94
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:32,060
If you look up the add instruction in this table,

95
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:33,720
it's written out very clearly here.

96
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,160
The add instruction

97
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:39,490
is exactly like this.

98
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:41,750
This is the opcode field.

99
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:44,410
This is function3 in 3 bits.

100
00:03:44,410 --> 00:03:46,590
This is function7 in 7 bits.

101
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:48,210
This is the first instruction.

102
00:03:48,210 --> 00:03:50,030
So it uses the simplest encoding.

103
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:50,350
All zeros.

104
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:53,070
That is to say, these three values

105
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:54,690
uniquely determine

106
00:03:54,690 --> 00:03:55,830
that this is an add instruction.

107
00:03:55,830 --> 00:03:57,490
Pay attention to this, everyone.

108
00:03:57,490 --> 00:03:58,410
Uniquely determined.

109
00:03:58,410 --> 00:03:59,130
This is an add instruction.

110
00:03:59,130 --> 00:04:03,660
Then RS1, RS2.

111
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:04,660
We just covered this earlier,

112
00:04:04,660 --> 00:04:06,040
the concept of source.

113
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:08,160
RD is the destination.

114
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,520
It's used to store the result.

115
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,300
Very simple.

116
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:11,980
This is an add instruction.

117
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:19,060
Now, let's briefly

118
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:21,320
use this instruction as an example again

119
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:22,060
and take a look at

120
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:23,660
how we

121
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:26,340
Once we've sorted that out,

122
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:27,540
what does the final encoded

123
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:28,420
machine instruction

124
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:29,380
actually look like?

125
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:33,070
So just now, by looking it up in the table,

126
00:04:33,070 --> 00:04:33,610
we already know

127
00:04:33,610 --> 00:04:34,950
the OPCODE is 0011,

128
00:04:34,950 --> 00:04:36,090
and these

129
00:04:36,090 --> 00:04:39,550
were found

130
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:42,890
from the table just now.

131
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:45,190
That leaves RSE2,

132
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:47,070
RSE, and RD — these three fields,

133
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:48,730
and we still don't know what to fill in, right?

134
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:50,970
So let's assume our command looks like this.

135
00:04:50,970 --> 00:04:52,750
Based on the explanation we just gave,

136
00:04:52,750 --> 00:04:55,710
we said 5 corresponds to D,

137
00:04:55,710 --> 00:04:57,330
6 to R1, R2.

138
00:04:57,330 --> 00:04:57,990
So what does that tell us?

139
00:04:57,990 --> 00:05:01,830
It tells us the value placed in this RD is what number?

140
00:05:01,830 --> 00:05:03,130
It has to be 5.

141
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:04,470
Think about it, right?

142
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:06,610
What's stored in that RSE?

143
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:07,610
It's definitely 6.

144
00:05:07,610 --> 00:05:09,030
Then 7.

145
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:11,030
S27.

146
00:05:11,030 --> 00:05:12,270
So,

147
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:15,870
but ultimately, what gets stored in the computer,

148
00:05:15,870 --> 00:05:16,910
is definitely not an integer,

149
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:18,010
not a decimal number.

150
00:05:18,010 --> 00:05:19,150
It definitely has to be turned into something — what is it?

151
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:20,770
Binary, right?

152
00:05:20,770 --> 00:05:22,010
Now, binary,

153
00:05:22,010 --> 00:05:23,510
you know, that is,

154
00:05:23,510 --> 00:05:25,630
let's convert it ourselves.

155
00:05:25,630 --> 00:05:26,390
Do the conversion.

156
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:27,570
This is very simple, right?

157
00:05:27,570 --> 00:05:30,030
It's just 111.

158
00:05:30,030 --> 00:05:32,070
The moment you see it, you should know it's 7.

159
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:32,890
Right?

160
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:35,090
For example, in that command line of yours,

161
00:05:35,090 --> 00:05:35,970
Type that, what is it

162
00:05:35,970 --> 00:05:38,330
When you change mode

163
00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:39,810
What does that 777 mean

164
00:05:39,810 --> 00:05:40,610
Right?

165
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:42,010
Isn't it just 111?

166
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:42,630
111, right?

167
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:42,790
Isn't it?

168
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:44,830
These numbers should be somewhat

169
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:46,250
familiar to everyone

170
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:47,350
For example, that change mode

171
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:48,530
What does 664 mean?

172
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:50,110
What does 777 mean?

173
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:50,390
Right?

174
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:52,250
Of course, I'm getting off track here

175
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:54,510
So once we have this string of numbers

176
00:05:54,510 --> 00:05:56,130
We basically know

177
00:05:56,130 --> 00:05:57,350
What's in this memory

178
00:05:57,350 --> 00:05:58,470
is just this thing

179
00:05:58,470 --> 00:06:00,030
But ultimately we still need to know

180
00:06:00,030 --> 00:06:00,590
where it is in memory

181
00:06:00,590 --> 00:06:01,310
How are they laid out?

182
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:01,510
Right?

183
00:06:01,510 --> 00:06:02,690
So usually we'll also

184
00:06:02,690 --> 00:06:05,530
take this binary, which looks pretty ugly,

185
00:06:05,530 --> 00:06:06,250
and for most of us,

186
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:08,670
we like to group it in eight bits, eight bits,

187
00:06:08,670 --> 00:06:09,410
and split it up,

188
00:06:09,410 --> 00:06:11,030
and then finally convert it into a

189
00:06:11,030 --> 00:06:12,230
hexadecimal number

190
00:06:12,230 --> 00:06:13,510
so we can work with it,

191
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:15,330
so we can do this,

192
00:06:15,330 --> 00:06:17,290
to read it,

193
00:06:17,290 --> 00:06:19,510
or to manipulate it,

194
00:06:19,510 --> 00:06:19,710
right?

195
00:06:19,710 --> 00:06:20,690
in programming.

196
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:22,750
So in the end, we know that

197
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:25,310
this add x5 x6 x7

198
00:06:25,310 --> 00:06:26,290
assembly instruction,

199
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:29,170
let's say your assembly instruction,

200
00:06:29,170 --> 00:06:32,070
then after it's processed by the assembler,

201
00:06:32,070 --> 00:06:33,530
It's essentially something like this:

202
00:06:33,530 --> 00:06:34,690
a binary number.

203
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:37,090
Now, with these binary numbers,

204
00:06:37,090 --> 00:06:38,430
how are they laid out in memory?

205
00:06:38,430 --> 00:06:42,110
This brings us to another issue:

206
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:43,850
