00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:28,720
As for the make debug command,

289
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,800
what it actually does inside,

290
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:32,360
basically, it starts up QEMU,

291
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:35,040
and then,

292
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:37,680
it takes the file we compiled and generated,

293
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:39,160
this file,

294
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:43,360
and through the -kernel parameter,

295
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:44,620
it loads it up.

296
00:09:44,620 --> 00:09:45,660
At the same time,

297
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:46,580
please note,

298
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:47,440
there's a -S here,

299
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:48,560
a capital S.

300
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:49,140
This one,

301
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:50,620
Let me also briefly explain

302
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:53,060
what QEMU actually is over here

303
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:53,800
since we haven't covered it yet

304
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:54,540
QEMU,

305
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:56,760
it's actually this thing right here

306
00:09:56,760 --> 00:09:59,260
Last time I probably went through this part a bit too fast

307
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:00,360
and just glossed over it in one go

308
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:01,320
so let me go over it again for you

309
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,060
This thing here

310
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:03,220
actually serves as a

311
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:05,520
system-level emulator for QEMU

312
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:07,080
Because if we look back for a moment,

313
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:07,580
you all remember, right?

314
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:09,620
QEMU has two running modes

315
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:12,840
One is called user mode

316
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:15,220
We generally use user mode to run a

317
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:17,120
executable program

318
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:19,440
The kind we're running now,

319
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:21,120
compiled from assembly instructions,

320
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:22,280
strictly speaking,

321
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:23,340
It's actually a piece of logic code.

322
00:10:23,340 --> 00:10:25,120
It can't be run on an operating system

323
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:26,080
directly.

324
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:27,440
If you try to run it,

325
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:28,860
directly under an operating system,

326
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:30,680
for example, running it directly on Linux,

327
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:32,840
it won't execute.

328
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:34,860
What we're actually doing is running this code directly in a logical sense,

329
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:37,080
so we launch it in system mode.

330
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:40,700
So that's why we start it in system mode.

331
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:44,600
system is the 32-bit RISC-V machine simulator,

332
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:46,740
and then we pass in some parameters.

333
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:50,820
-nographic basically means,

334
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:53,160
graphic, as we know, means graphics or images,

335
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,060
so it means it doesn't start a graphical interface.

336
00:10:55,060 --> 00:10:59,520
And then -smp means we only start a single core,

337
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:00,880
so basically just one hart.

338
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:04,200
And the machine we start

339
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:08,420
is of the machine type called virt,

340
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:09,360
that is, the simulator.

341
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:13,600
It simulates a device type called VRT.

342
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:14,480
What this device type is,

343
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:16,300
later when we talk about the operating system,

344
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:17,060
we'll look at it in detail.

345
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:18,560
For now, just having a simple understanding is enough.

346
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:20,740
Anyway, through this approach,

347
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:23,000
we get the emulator running.

348
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,660
It's essentially like a hart is up and running.

349
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:26,220
Then on this hart we run

350
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:30,320
the assembly instructions we compiled and generated,

351
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:31,560
the machine instructions.

352
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:34,200
So this is QEMU.

353
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:37,720
First, we launch a QEMU emulator.

354
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:39,120
And when we launch it,

355
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:41,300
after adding the -s and -S options,

356
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:42,820
what effect does it achieve?

357
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:44,560
Well, let's review for a moment.

358
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:45,320
We talked about this before,

359
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:47,160
this kind of debugging method

360
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:49,380
is called remote debugging.

361
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:52,940
Because the previous one, I'm not sure

362
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:55,360
Maybe let's briefly review that part again

363
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:58,320
That is to say, on which diagram

364
00:11:58,320 --> 00:12:04,950
On the one with the crossing

365
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:26,890
On this diagram

366
00:12:26,890 --> 00:12:31,860
Don't move it

367
00:12:31,860 --> 00:12:38,400
On this diagram

368
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:41,210
Everyone still remembers, right?

369
00:12:41,210 --> 00:12:41,870
Let's run it

370
00:12:41,870 --> 00:12:48,420
That is, we said that QEMU and GDB work together to debug

371
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:51,840
So we're actually getting to today's runtime environment

372
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:55,060
We're actually using a remote debugging approach

373
00:12:55,060 --> 00:12:55,600
Why?

374
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:58,740
Because it's a cross development and debugging environment

375
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:05,200
Our target machine and the machine running GDB

376
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:07,600
You can actually think of them as two different machines

377
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:09,580
It's just that our target machine

378
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:10,700
is currently an emulator

379
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:11,260
That is QEMU

380
00:13:11,260 --> 00:13:13,180
QEMU

381
00:13:13,180 --> 00:13:18,040
It emulates a RISC-V machine,

382
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:22,340
while our GDB is actually running on an x86 machine.

383
00:13:22,340 --> 00:13:22,600
Right?

384
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:24,280
This is a concept you need to grasp,

385
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:26,340
which is the cross development and debugging environment.

386
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:28,920
So when we run this QEMU,

387
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:30,520
if we pass the -s -S flags,

388
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:33,320
especially the -s option,

389
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:35,300
it will actually automatically start a GDB server

390
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:37,700
for you inside QEMU.

391
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:41,140
This diagram is explaining exactly that.

392
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:44,060
Between GDB and the GDB server,

393
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:45,980
we need to connect over a network.

394
00:13:45,980 --> 00:13:47,040
It's just that right now,

395
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:49,160
the emulator and our GDB

396
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:51,160
are both actually running on the same

397
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:52,220
Ubuntu machine, right?

398
00:13:52,220 --> 00:13:54,160
So in practice, this network connection

399
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:55,100
is a local loopback,

400
00:13:55,100 --> 00:13:56,500
not a real one.

401
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:57,780
Of course, you can also actually run it

402
00:13:57,780 --> 00:13:58,720
on a different machine,

403
00:13:58,720 --> 00:13:59,300
and that works fine too.

404
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:00,380
For example, you could run this emulator

405
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:02,560
on another machine,

406
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:04,040
and then establish a connection over the network.

407
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:04,840
That works as well.

408
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:07,000
So what does this -S do?

409
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:07,840
It will actually

410
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:09,900
set up a network connection,

411
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:10,500
and moreover,

412
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:11,460
as a service,

413
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:13,040
as we say with sockets,

414
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:14,720
it definitely needs a port number, right?

415
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:15,940
You still remember that concept, right?

416
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:17,600
Well, with our -S,

417
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:18,780
it defaults to using

418
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:20,300
port number 1234

419
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:21,460
and starts up a service.

420
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:23,200
You can think of this GDB

421
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:24,860
right on this port 1234,

422
00:14:24,860 --> 00:14:27,740
waiting for GDB to initiate a connection to it.

423
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:29,260
This is basically

424
00:14:29,260 --> 00:14:31,940
how the Socket works.

425
00:14:31,940 --> 00:14:33,680
So after we launch GDB,

426
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:36,720
we connect to it through the Socket.

427
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:38,960
Once connected, we can debug on both sides.

428
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,500
That's the idea here,

429
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,280
which is the effect of the lowercase s.

430
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:45,000
Now, what about the uppercase S?

431
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,260
What effect does it actually have?

432
00:14:46,260 --> 00:14:48,260
It makes the program, well,

433
00:14:48,260 --> 00:14:50,980
once you run it under GDB,

434
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:53,160
it will actually stop there and wait for you.

435
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:55,280
Isn't that exactly the effect we want?

436
00:14:55,280 --> 00:14:56,040
If it runs off on its own,

437
00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:56,980
we'd have no way to debug it.

438
00:14:56,980 --> 00:14:57,520
Think about it — isn't that right?

439
00:14:57,520 --> 00:15:00,300
So if we put the whole thing together,

440
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:02,100
we need to include these two options.

441
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:05,720
Just tell QML to run in this mode, and that's it.

442
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:06,640
Let me explain to everyone

443
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:09,660
what exactly is going on in the lab we're doing right now.

444
00:15:09,660 --> 00:15:13,590
So this is why you see it here,

445
00:15:13,590 --> 00:15:16,350
the reason we launch it this way.

446
00:15:16,350 --> 00:15:18,790
Now, the next symbol means that

447
00:15:18,790 --> 00:15:23,630
we will take the QML program process

448
00:15:23,630 --> 00:15:24,190
and do what with it?

449
00:15:24,190 --> 00:15:25,330
Send it to the background,

450
00:15:25,330 --> 00:15:25,550
right?

451
00:15:25,550 --> 00:15:27,170
Once it's in the background,

452
00:15:27,170 --> 00:15:28,470
we can then launch a

453
00:15:28,470 --> 00:15:29,430
local client —

454
00:15:29,430 --> 00:15:30,850
that's the left part

455
00:15:30,850 --> 00:15:31,470
of that diagram from earlier.

456
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:32,670
This client

457
00:15:32,670 --> 00:15:34,150
will be loaded on top of our

458
00:15:34,150 --> 00:15:35,850
compiled and generated ERF file.

459
00:15:35,850 --> 00:15:36,950
And then,

460
00:15:36,950 --> 00:15:38,130
just a quick note for everyone.

461
00:15:38,130 --> 00:15:39,750
Now, this -Q option,

462
00:15:39,750 --> 00:15:41,130
actually just stands for quiet.

463
00:15:41,130 --> 00:15:42,090
You can go check

464
00:15:42,090 --> 00:15:42,790
the manual yourselves.

465
00:15:42,790 --> 00:15:43,650
It's very simple:

466
00:15:43,650 --> 00:15:46,050
it avoids this—

467
00:15:46,050 --> 00:15:47,150
you know that when GDB

468
00:15:47,150 --> 00:15:47,670
starts up,

469
00:15:47,670 --> 00:15:49,250
it prints a bunch of garbage up front,

470
00:15:49,250 --> 00:15:49,990
and this gets rid of that,

471
00:15:49,990 --> 00:15:51,630
so it doesn't clutter our screen.

472
00:15:51,630 --> 00:15:52,690
And then,

473
00:15:52,690 --> 00:15:53,410
let me focus on

474
00:15:53,410 --> 00:15:54,110
this -x.

475
00:15:54,110 --> 00:15:55,290
With -x,

476
00:15:55,290 --> 00:15:58,310
it actually takes an argument

477
00:15:58,310 --> 00:16:01,150
that lets it run a configuration script.

478
00:16:01,150 --> 00:16:02,530
This configuration script,

479
00:16:02,530 --> 00:16:03,930
we've already written it for you,

480
00:16:03,930 --> 00:16:07,370
and it's a file called gdb init.

481
00:16:07,370 --> 00:16:09,030
In this file,

482
00:16:09,030 --> 00:16:10,350
it actually runs a bunch of

483
00:16:10,350 --> 00:16:11,530
the debug commands we use all the time.

484
00:16:11,530 --> 00:16:13,930
Because as you know,

485
00:16:13,930 --> 00:16:16,530
after you start up gdb,

486
00:16:16,530 --> 00:16:18,470
you'd have to type these commands by hand

487
00:16:18,470 --> 00:16:20,470
to connect to the server side,

488
00:16:20,470 --> 00:16:22,930
especially this target remote 1234,

489
00:16:22,930 --> 00:16:24,630
which basically acts as

490
00:16:24,630 --> 00:16:27,030
a client connecting to that GDB server.

491
00:16:27,030 --> 00:16:28,050
See what I mean?

492
00:16:28,050 --> 00:16:30,170
Having to type them every time is kind of annoying, right?

493
00:16:30,170 --> 00:16:32,330
So you can take these commands you use often

494
00:16:32,330 --> 00:16:33,830
and write them into a config script,

495
00:16:33,830 --> 00:16:35,550
and then at that spot we just saw,

496
00:16:35,550 --> 00:16:37,730
right here,

497
00:16:37,730 --> 00:16:39,970
tell GDB about it through this argument.

498
00:16:39,970 --> 00:16:41,190
Then when GDB starts up,

499
00:16:41,190 --> 00:16:43,270
it will automatically read that config script,

500
00:16:43,270 --> 00:16:45,450
and run these commands by default for you.

501
00:16:45,450 --> 00:16:46,370
Understand what I mean?

502
00:16:46,370 --> 00:16:47,710
That way,

503
00:16:47,710 --> 00:16:49,590
it's convenient for us to just...

504
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:51,490
not type those commands ourselves every time.

505
00:16:51,490 --> 00:16:54,870
As for what each command means,

506
00:16:54,870 --> 00:16:57,310
I won't say too much here.

507
00:16:57,310 --> 00:16:58,970
Especially what B and C mean.

508
00:16:58,970 --> 00:17:01,450
Earlier this is the most basic GDB command requirement.

509
00:17:01,450 --> 00:17:02,430
This sets a breakpoint, right?

510
00:17:02,430 --> 00:17:03,410
You still remember?

511
00:17:03,410 --> 00:17:05,830
A breakpoint can be followed by a function name.

512
00:17:05,830 --> 00:17:07,490
We just talked about what this start is.

513
00:17:07,490 --> 00:17:13,940
This start is actually what's in our program later.

514
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:16,300
The first file from just now,

515
00:17:16,300 --> 00:17:17,360
everyone has seen it, right?

516
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:18,460
There's a start here.

517
00:17:18,460 --> 00:17:20,540
As I just said, this start is an address.

518
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:23,660
Setting a breakpoint means setting it at an address.

519
00:17:23,660 --> 00:17:23,960
Right?

520
00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:26,040
So this start actually tells us

521
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:26,980
We set the breakpoint right here,

522
00:17:26,980 --> 00:17:29,580
which is actually the very first instruction of our program.

523
00:17:29,580 --> 00:17:31,940
Now this one,

524
00:17:31,940 --> 00:17:34,560
this is for setting the breakpoint,

525
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:36,620
and this one connects to the server side.

526
00:17:36,620 --> 00:17:37,660
As for c, it just

527
00:17:37,660 --> 00:17:39,620
lets the program run.

528
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:40,060
continue

529
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:41,900
But when it runs, it actually doesn't really run,

530
00:17:41,900 --> 00:17:43,140
it just gets caught at the breakpoint.

531
00:17:43,140 --> 00:17:44,780
Then this one is actually

532
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:45,760
set design,

533
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:46,160
design,

534
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:47,020
which actually means

535
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:48,840
setting it to single-step debugging mode,

536
00:17:48,840 --> 00:17:51,340
and it's single-step debugging at the assembly level.

537
00:17:51,340 --> 00:17:52,340
What this means is,

538
00:17:52,340 --> 00:17:54,480
these few commands

539
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:55,020
are actually just

540
00:17:55,020 --> 00:17:57,640
because when we debug,

541
00:17:57,640 --> 00:17:59,660
Don't you have to keep checking the registers? Right?

542
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:00,260
Right?

543
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:02,880
If you have to look at the registers every single time,

544
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:03,600
that can be a bit of a hassle.

545
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:04,280
So what we do is

546
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:06,880
use the display command every time,

547
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:08,240
so that during execution,

548
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:09,620
every time you single-step,

549
00:18:09,620 --> 00:18:11,040
it will automatically print out the values of these registers for you.

550
00:18:11,040 --> 00:18:12,060
It'll print them out for you automatically.

551
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:14,820
Because the setup we're demoing to you

552
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:15,840
is not that kind of graphical interface.

553
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:16,760
If it were a graphical interface,

554
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:17,980
you'd probably find it pretty nice to use,

555
00:18:17,980 --> 00:18:19,220
you know, the kind where

556
00:18:19,220 --> 00:18:21,460
one window shows the registers,

557
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:21,860
which you can watch,

558
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:23,720
and another window shows something else, right?

559
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:24,880
Right now we just have one shell,

560
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:27,260
so this is the way we handle it.

561
00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:28,560
You see, this is also

562
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:31,920
Of course, if you want to do it a bit nicer,

563
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:34,580
you could set up a graphical interface to debug it,

564
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:36,280
but on our side,

565
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:38,100
we won't demonstrate that here.

566
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:40,880
If any of you are interested, feel free to try it out on your own,

567
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:41,660
and if you manage to get it working,

